{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_thrmc7blo65tlbqvzt6l7bitrem47obetv57v3dkm4maq42reatq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 6 file pairs audited line-by-line, plus a differential re-run of all 24 sha2/shake configurations (12 sha2 × 12 shake) against the C `.so`.\n\n## Bottom line: no real divergences found in any of the six files.\n\nEmpirical confirmation: `scripts/run_cfg.sh` for all 24 sha2/shake configs → `SYMBOLS-OK` (exact symbol parity with `libc_sphincs.so`) and 9/9 test binaries passing, 0 failures. The harness's `obuf(n) = vec![0xA5; n + 96]` slack canary plus whole-buffer comparison means the exact bug class in your example (C writing more bytes than the Rust wrapper exposes) is directly covered for `sha256`/`sha512`/`sha256_inc_finalize`/`sha512_inc_finalize`/`SPX_mgf1_256`/`SPX_mgf1_512`/`shake256`/`shake256_inc_squeeze`/`shake256_squeezeblocks`/`SPX_prf_addr`/`SPX_thash`/`SPX_hash_message`.\n\n### FFI signature/length verification (all correct)\n\n| C prototype | Rust wrapper | verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sha256(out,in,size_t)` writes 32 B (`sha2.c:553`) | `sha2.rs:443` `from_raw_parts_mut(out, 32)` | OK |\n| `sha512(out,in,size_t)` writes 64 B (`sha2.c:603`) | `sha2.rs:449` `…, 64` | OK |\n| `sha256_inc_finalize` writes 32 B into `out`, state 40 B | `sha2.rs:429-431` 32/40/inlen | OK |\n| `sha512_inc_finalize` writes 64 B, state 72 B | `sha2.rs:436-438` 64/72/inlen | OK |\n| `sha256/512_inc_init` state 40/72 (`sha2.c:475,484`) | `sha2.rs:409,413` | OK |\n| `mgf1_256/512(out, unsigned long outlen, …)` (`sha2.h:33,37`) | `sha2.rs:454,460` `core::ffi::c_ulong`, out slice = `outlen` | OK |\n| `thash(…, unsigned int inblocks, …)` (`thash.h:11`) | `sha2_thash.rs:143`, `shake_thash.rs:46` `c_uint`; out = `SPX_N`, in = `inblocks*SPX_N` | OK |\n| `gen_message_random/hash_message(… unsigned long long mlen …)` (`hash.h:18,24`) | `sha2_hash.rs:188,206`, `shake_hash.rs:82,101` `c_ulonglong` | OK |\n| `shake256*(… size_t …)`, `uint64_t *s_inc` (`fips202.h:21-27,32`) | `fips202.rs:312-339`: `usize`; **26** lanes for the `_inc_*` family, **25** for `absorb`/`squeezeblocks` | OK (matches which lanes C touches) |\n| `spx_ctx` across FFI | `context.rs:9` `#[repr(C)]`, field set cfg-gated identically to `context.h:12-20` | OK |\n\n### Items I checked and judged benign (with locations)\n\n1. **`sha2.rs:236` / `sha2.c:513`** (and `sha2.rs:281` / `sha2.c:561`): C `uint64_t bytes = load_bigendian_64(state+32) + inlen` wraps; Rust uses plain `+`. Also `sha2.rs:223,230` vs `sha2.c:497,506` (`bytes += 64*inblocks`). **Benign** — release builds wrap identically; a debug panic needs a ≥2^64-byte message counter. `wrapping_add` would be more faithful but is unobservable.\n2. **`thash_sha2_robust.c:30` vs `sha2_thash.rs:26`**: C sizes the VLA `SPX_N + SPX_SHA256_OUTPUT_BYTES(32) + inblocks*SPX_N`, Rust `SPX_N + SPX_SHA256_ADDR_BYTES(22) + inblocks*SPX_N`. C only ever writes/hashes `buf[SPX_N .. SPX_N+22+inblocks*SPX_N]` (`thash_sha2_robust.c:42,45-46`) — the extra 10 bytes are dead. Identical hashed byte range. **Benign.** (The C SHA-512 twin at `:56` already uses 22.)\n3. **`sha2.c:697-700` vs `sha2.rs:393-400`**: `#if SPX_SHA512` → `#[cfg(spx_sha512)] { if SPX_SHA512 {…} }`. `SPX_SHA512 = cfg!(spx_sha512)` (`params.rs:65`), so the inner `if` is always true inside the block. **Benign.**\n4. **`hash_sha2.c:11` (`#if SPX_N >= 24`) vs `sha2_hash.rs:14/24` (`cfg(spx_sha512)`)**: different selector, same result — verified against all six `params-sphincs-sha2-*.h` (128s/128f: N=16, SPX_SHA512=0; 192*/256*: N≥24, SPX_SHA512=1) and `build.rs:79`. **Benign.**\n5. **`hash_sha2.c:114`** `shaX(buf, buf, …)` aliases in place; **`sha2_hash.rs:100-101`** copies to `tmp` first. Equivalent because `sha*_inc_finalize` writes `out` only after all input is consumed (`sha2.c:553`). **Benign.**\n6. **`sha2.c:290`** `crypto_hashblocks_sha512` is declared `int` but returns `unsigned long long inlen` (truncating); Rust `sha2.rs:139` returns `()`. Return value is unused by both callers. **Benign.**\n7. **`fips202.rs:43-81`** replaces the fully unrolled `KeccakF1600_StatePermute` (`fips202.c:71-332`) with the standard round-loop form. Verified equivalent: lane indexing `s[x+5y]` matches (`fips202.c:88-112` vs `fips202.rs:48`), and the differential tests compare the *whole 25-lane state* after `shake256_absorb`/`squeezeblocks` (`phase_b_primitives.rs:377,383`) across all 12 shake configs. Round constants, `K256`, `K512`, `IV_256`, `IV_512` diffed programmatically — all exact. **Benign.**\n8. **`fips202.rs:183-200, 256-293`**: `shake128*`/`sha3_256*`/`sha3_512*` are implemented in Rust but deliberately **not** exported. Confirmed correct: `fips202.c` only *defines* the SHAKE-256 family (`fips202.c:523-598`); `nm -D` on `libc_sphincs.so` shows exactly the 7 `shake256*` symbols. **No divergence.**\n9. The uninitialized-`padded[128]`/`padded[256]` in `sha256_inc_finalize`/`sha512_inc_finalize` (`sha2.c:512,560`) — I verified every byte in the hashed range is written in both branches of both functions, so Rust's zero-init (`sha2.rs:235,280`) cannot diverge. Off-by-one boundaries `< 56`/`< 112`, and the `padded[56..64]`/`[119..128]`/`[120..128]`/`[247..256]` shift ladders match byte-for-byte.\n10. `INBLOCKS`: C `& -SPX_SHAX_BLOCK_BYTES` (`hash_sha2.c:141`) vs Rust `& !(BLOCK-1)` (`sha2_hash.rs:106`) — identical for power-of-two BLOCK; checked numerically for all three (N,PK,BLOCK) triples → 1 in every case.\n\n### One non-divergence issue worth flagging to the user\n\n`work/test_sha2_128s_simple.log:53` — `phase_b_pipeline` SIGSEGVs when cargo runs tests multi-threaded. It passes with `--test-threads=1` and passes 3/3 times multi-threaded under `RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216`. This is a **2 MiB test-thread stack overflow** (large stack buffers / C VLAs in the `crypto_sign`/`treehash` path), i.e. a test-harness artifact, **not** a translation divergence. Suggest adding `RUST_MIN_STACK` or `--test-threads=1` to `scripts/run_cfg.sh`.\n\nNo files were modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3043,"cache_read_input_tokens":147588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3043,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Q8SrE9pL15ifLiS6yGJFGF","session_id":"6db5455e-f768-477c-9943-e81d8027edae","uuid":"4c322002-8f00-4257-9e17-8999a51fda39","timestamp":"2026-08-29T00:38:26.857Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review sha2+shake translations"}
